WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.514
Before this service takes traffic, three caches must be warm.

2
00:00:06.514 --> 00:00:10.171
The thread that opens the port must wait for three loaders.

3
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:13.079
A CountDownLatch is a gate with a counter.

4
00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:14.322
It starts at three.

5
00:00:14.322 --> 00:00:17.329
The opener calls await, and stops at the gate.

6
00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:20.292
Each loader calls countDown when it is done.

7
00:00:20.292 --> 00:00:21.320
Three, two, one.

8
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.567
At zero, the gate opens, and every thread waiting there goes on.

9
00:00:26.733 --> 00:00:29.035
Now a late thread calls await.

10
00:00:29.035 --> 00:00:30.032
Does it wait?

11
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:31.827
No.

12
00:00:31.827 --> 00:00:33.342
The gate stays open.

13
00:00:33.342 --> 00:00:37.280
The count never goes back up, and there is no reset.

14
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.795
A latch is one-shot.

15
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.537
Now four workers each simulate one day of exposure.

16
00:00:42.537 --> 00:00:46.352
Nobody may start day two until all four finish day one.

17
00:00:46.533 --> 00:00:49.186
A CyclicBarrier counts threads, not events.

18
00:00:49.186 --> 00:00:52.456
Each worker calls await at the line, and waits there.

19
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.468
The fourth to arrive runs the barrier action once, here adding up the day.

20
00:00:57.468 --> 00:00:59.376
Then all four go on together.

21
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:00.246
Day two.

22
00:01:00.246 --> 00:01:03.072
Is the gate still open, like the latch?

23
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:04.890
No.

24
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:06.980
It closed again behind them.

25
00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.815
The barrier resets itself every round.

26
00:01:09.815 --> 00:01:11.605
That is the cyclic part.

27
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.666
A Phaser is a barrier whose group can change.

28
00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:16.767
A party can deregister and leave.

29
00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:20.588
Each phase then waits only for the parties still registered.

30
00:01:20.733 --> 00:01:24.442
Last, the card issuer throttles above 20 calls at once.

31
00:01:24.442 --> 00:01:26.195
A Semaphore holds permits.

32
00:01:26.195 --> 00:01:28.689
Say three, to keep the picture small.

33
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:30.402
acquire takes a permit.

34
00:01:30.402 --> 00:01:33.808
Only a thread holding a permit may call the issuer.

35
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.228
The fourth thread finds the tray empty, so it waits.

36
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:42.207
When a call finishes, release gives the permit back, and the waiting thread takes it.

37
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.892
Now a thread that never took a permit calls release.

38
00:01:45.892 --> 00:01:46.765
What happens?

39
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:50.527
The semaphore just adds a permit.

40
00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:51.990
Permits have no owner.

41
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.713
Now four threads fit, and the limit has quietly drifted.

42
00:01:57.267 --> 00:02:00.246
Side by side: a latch waits for events, once.

43
00:02:00.246 --> 00:02:02.961
A barrier waits for a group, every round.

44
00:02:02.961 --> 00:02:05.543
A semaphore limits how many are inside.

45
00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:10.999
In code, each one is a single constructor, in Java and in Kotlin.

46
00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:15.919
The rule: count events with a latch, meet with a barrier, limit with a semaphore.

47
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:18.394
Quick check.

48
00:02:18.394 --> 00:02:24.879
Four workers settle twelve monthly batches, and each month must finish before the next one starts.

49
00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:26.004
Latch or barrier?

50
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:28.884
A barrier.

51
00:02:28.884 --> 00:02:32.032
A latch would stay open after the first month.
